  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nMy primary approach leverages the inherent structure of step sequences by combining multiple candidate profiles optimized using linear programming. This method helps prevent settling into local minima by ensuring diverse exploration. I improve upon the previous algorithm by:  \n1. Using more aggressive initialization with non-symmetric edge profiles to lower baseline peaks faster.  \n2. Implementing frequency-domain analysis early (via FFT) to guide strategic zero placements.  \n3. Adding an adaptive envelope optimization loop that iteratively improves convolution decay patterns.  \n4. Utilizing a combination of simplex-normalized uniform peaks and edge-falloff optimization\n\nMathematical specification:\nWe minimize J(a) = (2 \u00d7 n \u00d7 max(conv(a,a))) / (sum(a))\u00b2  \nover sequences in \u211d\u207a\u207f, maintained in normalized form (sum(a) = \u221a(2\u00d7n)).  \nKey formulation:  \n- Build sparse convolution constraint matrix (MAP mode)  \n- Optimize over simplex-normalized combinations (Frank-Wolfe + proximal steps)  \n- Compute gradient as ratios of convolution positions to sequence sum  \n\nImplementation procedure:\ndef propose_candidate(budget=1800):\n    best_seq = None\n    best_score = float('inf')\n    candidate_base = {\n        'uniform_left': np.ones(n) * (np.sqrt(2*n)/n).reshape(-1,1),\n        'uniform_right': np.ones(n) * (np.sqrt(2*n)/n).reshape(-1,1),\n        'comb_peak': np.exp(-((np.arange(n)-n//2)**2)) / (np.sqrt(2*n))\n    }  \n    # Phase 1: Aggressive initializer with 2\u00d7n edge variations  \n    env_diversities = [(0.7, 0.3), (0.6, 0.4), (0.5, 0.5)]  \n    sample_size = 5  \n    for idx in range(sample_size):  \n        seq_to_optimize = initial_candidates[idx]  \n        envelope_level = env_diversities[idx]  \n        new_seq = apply_envelope(seq_to_optimize, envelope_level)  \n        improve(new_seq)  # with adaptive Frank-Wolfe using linprog  \n\n    # Post-process edge placement via frequency-domain analysis  \n    def optimize_edges(initial_seq, max_time=180):  \n        using_fft_time = 0.0  \n        fluctuation_matrix = full_cycle_fft(initial_seq)  \n        duration = compute_peak_mask(fluctuation_matrix)  \n        time_allocation = max_time * 0.7  # use FFT time-wise resource allocation  \n        try:  \n            scaled=time_allocation  \n            return polish_sequence(initial_seq, scaled)\n        except TimeOverflow:  \n            fallback_ff = sequential_reduction(initial_seq)  \n            return fallback_ff  \n\n    # Phase 4: Visual verification with secondary FFT peak limit  \n    verified_final_score = evaluate_sequence(best_seq)  \n    generate_visual_report(best_seq, \"Found optimal envelope decay!\")  \n\n    return best_seq  \n\nKey definitions:  \n- Full cycle FFT: np.fft.irfft(np.conj(np.fft.rfft(x)) * np.fft.rfft(x))  \n- Envelope application: np.maximum(0, initial_seq * envelope_level[:, None])  \n- Simplex normalization: sequence = sequence / sum(sequence) * np.sqrt(2*n)  \n\nSearch choices and useful variations:  \nWe explore adaptive edge-normalization variations with triggering:  \n1. When edge peaks dominate the central convolution (exceeding J(a) threshold)  \n2. When multiple sample combinations consistently improve  \n3. When sequence length exceeds 512 (FFT optimization recommends larger edge placements)  \n\nThe coder must implement:  \n- generate_envelope_sequences(length, sample_count): returns array of normalized profiles  \n- apply_envelope_to_base(base_seq, envelope_ratio): nonnegative combinatorial blending  \n- phase_time_allocation(total_budget, len_to_try): adaptive FFT sizing  \n- verify_final_objective(candidate_obj, name): statistical validation  \n- fallback_simplex(sequence): basic solution if advanced methods stall  \n\nComputebudget and robustness:  \nTime is allocated as:  \n- 40% Training non-symmetric edges  \n- 40% Optimization using projected subgradients and LPs  \n- 20% Random perturbation exploration  \nTotal estimated operations: O(n\u00d7log n) via FFT wrappers.  \n\nFallback: Best sequence exists as a normalized sample (\\(\\sum a = \\sqrt{2n}\\)) meeting >0.01 min sum requirement.  \n\nValidation: Use provided evaluate_sequence for all end-candidate checks, verify results are finite and comparable.",
